asy  compared to opening a human hospital the  requirements for a hospital have to be  astronomical but less red tape makes it  easier to open an animal hospital says  dr. Weinstein who used to run his own  clinic complete with x-ray facilities  and operating rooms  I believe it was 12 weeks from the time  we signed the lease to the time I saw my  first client try to do that in human  healthcare it would take at least 20  times as long to open a comparable human  hospital according to a California  Hospital Association estimate it can  take even longer in states like North  Carolina if you're an animal if you're a  dog or a cat there are eight animal  hospitals within close driving distance  of right where are we speaking right now  there's no hospital for people  something's wrong with that  this spot was supposed to be home to a  new hospital that would serve the  growing community of Holly Springs North  Carolina but state law stopped the  project anywhere in North Carolina it's  complicated state regulators have to  approve projects through a certificate  of need process under certificate of  need or con law if you want to build a  hospital you have to convince state  regulators that the hospital is needed  President Nixon signed the policy into  law because he thought the US had too  many hospitals he thought decreasing the  number of hospitals with lower  healthcare costs but things didn't work  out as planned so basically you ended up  with competitors a would be competitors  being kept out of the market so prices  started going up reason magazines ron  bailey writes about health care policy  he notes that con laws allow existing  hospitals to hold up plans for new  hospitals the existing hospitals go in  front of these government agencies and  say we do not need a new hospital we  don't need any competitors over there  we're taking fine care of the community  here this guy represents existing  hospitals in North Carolina think he  wants a new competitor we don't think  it's in the best interest of the  citizens of North Carolina and so what  you ended up with is a government  regulation that was designed to lower  the cost of health care actually  increasing the cost of healthcare  increasing cost and decreasing  competition consider hospitals that  focus on things like women's medicine or  heart disease states with con laws have  far fewer specialty hospitals compared  to states without such laws even though  both groups have roughly the same  population today 14 states have gotten  rid of their Kahn laws but 36 states  still have them and even when a new  hospital gets the green light from  regulators the fight is far from over  United Regional Medical Center has the  green light from the state to build a  brand new hospital but there's a lawsuit  to stop it guess who's been trying to  stop a new hospital from opening in  Manchester Tennessee to existing  hospitals they fought every step of the  plan for almost two years now frustrated  residents are protesting this 90 year  old lady has begun a petition drive for  a new hospital people resorting to  petitions and protests just because  existing hospitals are afraid of a  little competition there are no special  laws that lets Starbucks prevent other  coffee shops from opening for vanilla  one restaurant to keep other restaurants  away I would be very upset if we took  some of that entrepreneurial spirit out  of the veterinary profession dr.  Weinstein is glad vets don't have to  deal with regulations like con laws it's  great that veterinarians can be  entrepreneurs it allows veterans to be  small business people it allows veterans  to focus on service and care these are  the people who decide how many animal  hospitals there should be in veterinary  medicine we could have two practices  right next to one another and then it's  the consumer deciding to whom they want  to go if that way it's good enough for  these guys maybe it's good enough for us  too for reason TV